course: french
lesson: 27
title: Adjectives: after the noun, and agreeing
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In English the describing word comes first, "a black dog", but in French most describing words come after the noun: un chien noir.
explain: A small group of short, everyday ones go in front, and the trick BAGS helps you remember them: Beauty (beau, joli), Age (jeune, vieux, nouveau), Goodness (bon, mauvais) and Size (petit, grand, gros).
explain: Describing words also change to match their noun, like a chameleon changing colour: add -e for a feminine word and -s for a plural one - un chat noir, une souris noire, des chats noirs.
explain: Sometimes the change is loud: petit sounds "puh-TEE", but petite sounds "puh-TEET", because the e wakes up the t; and a few words change a lot, like beau into belle.
explain: Picture describing your family to a penpal: "Mon frère est petit, ma sœur est petite, et nos chiens sont très gentils" - every describing word dresses to match.
fact: Most adjectives go after the noun: un chien noir. BAGS adjectives go before: Beauty, Age, Goodness and Size, such as beau, joli, jeune, vieux, nouveau, bon, mauvais, petit, grand and gros.
fact: Adding -e for feminine often makes the last consonant heard: petit ("puh-TEE") becomes petite ("puh-TEET"); grand and grande, vert and verte work the same way.
fact: A size word goes before the noun and still agrees: une grande maison.
fact: Some adjectives change a lot: beau and belle (beautiful), nouveau and nouvelle (new), vieux and vieille (old).
fact: Adjectives agree in gender and number: noir, noire, noirs, noires, and all four sound the same.
fact: Adjectives ending in -eux become -euse in the feminine: heureux and heureuse (happy), sérieux and sérieuse.
fact: Some adjectives double their last letter before adding e: gros and grosse, bon and bonne, gentil and gentille.
fact: Nationality and colour adjectives go after the noun, and nationalities are written with a small letter: un garçon français, une fille anglaise.
fact: Adjectives agree even after est: il est petit, elle est petite.
fact: A noun keeps its gender every time: une pomme verte et une pomme rouge.
fact: Très means very and goes before the adjective: très bon, très grand, très joli.
exercise: Noah writes about a black dog. Which is right? | un chien noir | un noir chien | une chien noire | un chien noire
exercise: Which adjectives usually go BEFORE the noun? | BAGS words like petit and beau | Colour words like rouge and vert | Nationality words like français | All adjectives, just like English
exercise: Ava writes "une petit fille". What is right? | une petite fille | une petit fille | un petite fille | une fille petit
exercise: How does petite sound compared with petit? | The t at the end is heard | Exactly the same | The p at the start goes silent | It gets an extra "uh" sound
exercise: Kai writes "une maison grand". What is right? | une grande maison | une maison grande | un grand maison | une grand maisone
exercise: Tilly says "Ma chienne est beau." What is right? | Ma chienne est belle | Ma chienne est beaue | Ma chienne est beaux | Ma chienne est bel
exercise: What is the feminine of nouveau? | nouvelle | nouveaue | nouvelles | nouvelleau
exercise: Lily writes "les chiens noir". What is right? | les chiens noirs | les chiens noir | les chien noirs | les chiens noires
exercise: Daniel writes "une fille heureux". What is right? | une fille heureuse | une fille heureuxe | une fille heureux | une fille heureuses
exercise: Jake writes "une grose souris". What is right? | une grosse souris | une grose souris | une grosses souris | une gros souris
exercise: Which adjective doubles its last letter in the feminine? | bon, which becomes bonne | noir, which becomes noire | grand, which becomes grande | petit, which becomes petite
exercise: Olivia wants "a kind French boy". Which order is fine? | un garçon français gentil | un Français garçon gentil | un gentil Français garçon | un garçon Français gentil
exercise: How is "French" written in une fille française? | With a small f | With a capital F | With a capital F and no e | With a small f and no e
exercise: Charlie writes "Elle est petit" about his sister. What is right? | Elle est petite | Elle est petit | Elle est petits | Elle est une petit
exercise: Ruby writes "une pomme verte et un pomme rouge". What is wrong? | The second apple must be une | The colour verte should be vert | The colour rouge should be rougee | Nothing, apples can change gender
exercise: How do you say "a very good cake"? | un très bon gâteau | un bon très gâteau | un gâteau bon très | un très gâteau bon
test: Your mate from Manchester goes, "French is backwards, every adjective goes after, our kid." What's true? | Most go after, but BAGS words like grand go before | He's right, every single one goes after | No, every single one goes before | Only colours go after, everything else before
test: ★★ A new adjective, joli, describes une maison. Where does it go and how is it spelled? | Before, as jolie: une jolie maison | After, as joli: une maison joli | After, as jolie: une maison jolie | Before, as joli: une joli maison
test: Tilly from Leeds asks, "Belle? Like the princess?" What's the link? | The name Belle means beautiful | Belle means princess in French | Belle means bell, like a church bell | There's no link, it's just a name
test: ★★ Nathan describes his gran as "vieux" and his teacher shakes her head. Why? | Gran is feminine, so it is vieille | Vieux is rude, you should say jeune | Vieux only goes with animals | It should be vieuxe with an e
test: A cartoon shows four black cats, and the caption says "les chats noirs". Your brother says the adjective should not have an s, "cos colours don't count". Who's right? | The caption: noirs agrees | Your brother: colours never change | Neither: it should be les chats noires | Neither: it should be les chat noir
test: ★★★ Which phrase has every word right? | une grande maison blanche | une maison grande blanche | un grand maison blanche | une grande blanche maison
test: What would happen if you said "une fille heureux" to a French teacher? | She'd understand, but correct it to heureuse | She'd think you said a different word | She'd think the girl was sad | Nothing, it is completely correct
test: ★★ Jake from Bradford goes, "Two s's for a tiny mouse? Bruv, that's bare long." Which pair works like gros and grosse? | gentil and gentille | grand and grande | sérieux and sérieuse | petit and petite
test: ★★★ "Les ___ sont très gentilles." Which word fits the gap? | filles | garçons | chiens | chats
test: Ethan from Toronto says, "Eh, I'll say C'est un gâteau très bon." (eh = right?) What's the more natural French? | C'est un très bon gâteau | C'est un gâteau bon très | C'est un bon gâteau très | C'est très un bon gâteau
test: Your cousin writes "Elle est Anglaise" with a capital A. What's the fix? | anglaise with a small a | Anglais with no e | Anglaises with an s | Nothing, it's fine
exam: Identify the position customarily occupied by an adjective of colour in relation to the noun it qualifies. | After the noun | Before the noun | At the end of the sentence | At the beginning of the sentence
exam: Select the correctly inflected form of the adjective heureux when qualifying the feminine noun fille. | heureuse | heureuxe | heureux | heureuses
exam: Determine which of the following phrases conforms in all respects to the rules of position and agreement. | une petite fille | une fille petit | un petite fille | une petit fille
exam: Ascertain the feminine form of the adjective "beau". | belle | beaue | beaux | bel
